  "question": {
    "text": "Each of $a$ and $b$ can take values 1 or 2 with equal probability. The probability that the equation $a x^{2}+b x+1=0$ has real roots, is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "vSmvv",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "xHVvV",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{4}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Xc9jA",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{8}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "RWpeg",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{16}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The given equation\r\n$$\r\na x^{2}+b x+1=0\r\n$$\r\nhas real roots.\r\n$\\therefore$ Discriminant $(D) \\geq 0$\r\n$$\r\n\\Rightarrow \\quad b^{2}-4 a \\geq 0\r\n$$\r\nFrom Eq. (ii), we observe that\r\na has to be 1 and $b$ has to be 2 . \r\nSo, the required probability $=\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$",
    "image": null
  },
